{
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_name": "Becoming capable: leadership, foresight, and practical adulthood",
      "description": "Texturally, marching band is experienced as a training ground for concrete competencies—leadership, responsibility, time management, problem-solving, and thinking ahead to prevent issues. These are not abstract lessons but learned through repeated roles (section leader, drum major) and the ongoing work of coordinating with “all different types of people.” Structurally, capability is forged in the demands of performance culture: deadlines, precision, accountability, and peer coordination require the participant to act, decide, anticipate, and repair in real time. In whole-part reconciliation, the participant’s “tangible” skills and later adult work-life benefits are not separate outcomes; they are the continuation of a way of being practiced in band—competence formed through repeated responsibility under collective pressure."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Belonging and being held: band as ‘my people’",
      "description": "Texturally, band is lived as a social home—“my people” in hallways and classes, a group who would “have my back” simply through shared membership. Memories cluster around everyday rituals and recognition (eating out after football games, band kids elevated in homecoming), underscoring that belonging is enacted in small, repeated moments as much as in formal events. Structurally, this belonging is made possible by the organizational boundary of band: membership produces an assumed solidarity and a dependable social map inside the wider school and university. In whole-part reconciliation, the participant’s “intangible” sense of safety, loyalty, and identity is revealed as central rather than secondary; the skills and achievements gain meaning because they are embedded in a felt community that organizes social life and self-worth."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Bond through shared purpose and mutual dependence",
      "description": "Texturally, the participant describes an “underlying bond” arising from making music together, striving “to be good,” and knowing others are counting on one’s part while one also depends on theirs. In college, this intensifies because members “100% chose to be there,” making commitment itself a felt ingredient of connection. Structurally, the bond is produced by interdependence and collective stakes: the ensemble’s success is distributed across individuals, turning responsibility into relational glue. In whole-part reconciliation, friendship and musicianship are not parallel tracks; they interpenetrate—trust is built through coordinated effort, and musical excellence becomes a shared moral project that generates closeness."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Life shaped by mentorship and an unexpected web of continuity",
      "description": "Texturally, marching band is narrated as life-directing: it influences college choice through trust in a director’s guidance, reveals surprising institutional ties between high school and university, and becomes the context for meeting a spouse and a lifelong best friend. Moments of recognition (“boom there he was”) crystallize the sense that one’s path is threaded through relationships and shared networks. Structurally, identity formation occurs through guided pathways and social infrastructures—mentors, program reputations, and overlapping communities that quietly channel opportunity and belonging. In whole-part reconciliation, “marching band led me to who I am today” becomes coherent: the participant’s adult relationships, educational trajectory, and sense of meaning are not incidental benefits but the lived outcome of being carried along (and choosing into) a relational ecosystem."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Crossing cultures: from rule-bound discipline to self-governed autonomy",
      "description": "Texturally, the participant feels “shock” moving from high school’s strict commands and procedures to college band’s relaxed norms (phones out, soda in uniform). The body remembers discipline—standing at attention—while the new setting demands self-management rather than external enforcement: “you’re responsible for yourself… do the right thing or don’t.” Structurally, the transition is a shift in moral/organizational logic: control moves from explicit rules to internalized responsibility, and the participant must reinterpret what professionalism and commitment look like without constant surveillance. In whole-part reconciliation, this cultural dissonance is part of maturation: the earlier discipline becomes a foundation, while the later autonomy invites the participant to own values and choices rather than merely comply."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Visibility, marginalization, and collective agency: campaigning from the back to the front",
      "description": "Texturally, the flute section is experienced as routinely placed at the margins—top of the stands, back of the field—accompanied by the sting of feeling that “nobody really cared.” In response, the participant describes pride and enjoyment in organizing, campaigning for better placement, and using humor and “petty” solidarity—jokes and covert acts—to cope and resist. Structurally, this theme emerges from hierarchical staging and perceived status within the ensemble; spatial placement becomes symbolic of recognition and value. In whole-part reconciliation, advocacy is not merely a funny anecdote; it reveals how belonging coexists with contestation—band is both a home and a system with inequities, and the participant’s agency (and shared humor) becomes a way of reclaiming visibility and dignity within the group."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_name": "Dislocation and the re-making of competence",
      "description": "Texturally, Reid describes entering the Cavaliers as a newly-18-year-old from a high school program he loved but experienced as “different” and in transition—smaller, changing demographics, altered culture. The move into drum corps is felt as a sudden scale-shift (far beyond the “49 other brass” he had known), and as an immediate positional shift from being “one of the better” performers to someone whose effort is “not good enough.” Structurally, this dislocation is intensified by arriving a week late to spring training—starting from behind, trying to catch a moving train—so that identity and ability are re-evaluated under pressure. The experience is not merely learning new drill; it is learning a new world of expectation, comparison, and self-measurement where prior confidence no longer secures belonging."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Embodied overwhelm and the risk of being seen",
      "description": "Texturally, the experience condenses into an embodied moment: “crying beneath my sunglasses,” frustration turning physical, private tears held inside a public rehearsal space. He carries self-judgment and fear of inadequacy while trying to appear composed. Structurally, this is the phenomenology of initiation: the novice is exposed to high standards without the protective familiarity of known peers, routines, or reputational capital. The sunglasses become a boundary between inner collapse and outer performance, revealing how drum corps demands emotional containment even as it provokes emotional rupture."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Shared vulnerability as the birth of brotherhood",
      "description": "Texturally, relief arrives not through technical improvement but through confession and reciprocity: he tells veteran members (still strangers) about crying and frustration; they respond with their own stories of feeling “less than worthy.” In that exchange, “shared experience” becomes lived rather than slogan—strangers become “brothers,” and he feels “loved and seen.” Structurally, the bonding mechanism is mutual disclosure within a demanding environment: belonging is produced through narrative mirroring (“me too”), which transforms individual failure into collective human experience. Whole–part reconciliation: the very intensity that threatens identity also becomes the condition under which deep connection can form—hardness makes room for intimacy when vulnerability is met with care."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Rupture of the ‘we’: disruption, toxicity, and the loss of a common world",
      "description": "Texturally, COVID operates as a decisive rupture: 21 days without full-corps rehearsal becomes a breeding ground for blame, finger-pointing, and a “toxic” climate. The season ends in relational collapse—“we hated each other”—and he leaves declaring he never wants to return. Structurally, disrupted continuity (fragmented rehearsals, uncertainty, weakened shared routine) undermines the cohesion that normally stabilizes corps identity. When the practices that synchronize bodies and intentions disappear, social interpretation fills the gap: people search for causes, locate scapegoats, and convert stress into interpersonal threat. In whole–part terms, the cherished ideal of brotherhood is revealed as fragile—dependent on sustained, collective practice and a maintained sense of “in it together.”"
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "External judgment and competitive comparison as a poison to interior meaning",
      "description": "Texturally, a quote—“I didn’t know we were doing bad until someone told me we were”—reframes his experience: the felt reality of working can be overtaken by what others say it means. He recounts the shift after early poor results: staff and members move from internal focus to worrying about “what someone thought of our work,” and relationships become adversarial. Structurally, competition functions as an interpretive lens that can colonize the everyday: once external evaluation becomes the dominant horizon, members begin to measure one another as liabilities, and morale reorganizes around anxiety and status. Whole–part reconciliation: scores are said not to matter, yet they matter indirectly by changing how members treat each other; the true harm is not losing, but losing a shared interpretive frame that protects the ‘we.’"
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Misguided accountability: when discipline becomes hazing and scapegoating",
      "description": "Texturally, the shoe-polishing incident is recounted as an ethical break: a late-night line, inspection, public singling-out, and forced running at 11 p.m. after rehearsal—explicitly named “not acceptable.” The event produces “enemies” and a vocabulary of blame (“you’re holding us back”). Structurally, under competitive stress and cultural instability, leadership attempts to restore control through symbolic punishment, converting ‘accountability’ into spectacle. This reorganizes the group from mutual responsibility to surveillance and humiliation, making cohesion impossible. Whole–part reconciliation: the corps’ stated identity (brotherhood) collides with practices that enact hierarchy and exclusion; the absence of a positive shared experience is not neutral—it actively fractures the social body."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Endurance, limited control, and choosing to stay for cultural repair",
      "description": "Texturally, Reid links experiences across contexts: as a high school brass captain during a season that missed finals, he later understands he “could have only controlled so much.” Similarly, he remains with the Cavaliers through “three really rough years” because he believes change is possible and wants to help make it. Structurally, this theme reveals an evolving stance toward agency: moving from self-blame to a more complex understanding of systems, leadership, and collective morale. Temporally, meaning is not fixed at a single moment; it is reinterpreted across years, with staying becoming a moral and relational commitment rather than a simple pursuit of success."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Repair through aligned leadership and relational care",
      "description": "Texturally, the later experience carries signs of repair: by 2025 the group is “mostly on the same page,” and having Cavaliers alumni on staff feels “special,” easing connection through a positive lineage of experience. Most vividly, mentorship becomes enacted care: Jeremy stays with him through “almost half of the visual block” while he sobs and continues checking in after the season. Structurally, repair happens when leadership embodies the values it preaches—when shared experience is not used to demand toughness but to offer understanding. Whole–part reconciliation: even when endings are “ruined” by personal life events outside corps dynamics, the presence of attuned care preserves the meaning of the activity as life-giving rather than merely demanding."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Shared experience as the ground of meaning—capable of blessing or harm",
      "description": "Texturally, “shared experience” is repeatedly named as the core of marching arts: it is what makes drum corps “everything,” what turns strangers into family, and what differentiates positive hardship from destructive conflict. He contrasts Langham’s “family” (people sustaining meaning despite weaker competitive outcomes) with Cavaliers seasons where shared experience turns negative or disappears. Structurally, shared experience functions as the condition that organizes all other dimensions—performance, competition, leadership, discipline, and belonging. When it is positive, difficulty becomes connective and identity-expanding; when it is negative or fractured, difficulty becomes isolating and corrosive. The whole of Reid’s account reconciles as a single interpretive claim: the decisive variable is not excellence versus failure, but whether the group can sustain an authentic ‘we’ through hardship."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_name": "Mentored legitimacy within a precariously intimate bond",
      "description": "Texturally, the high-school world is remembered through a “very personal” relationship with the director—late-night, after-game conversations in the band office where “life chats” (sometimes musical, often not) provided “straight up” guidance about the future. Structurally, this mentorship is made possible by access to an invested adult inside the band space, yet it is also framed by adult hindsight that the closeness “may have appeared to be inappropriate,” requiring the participant to actively mark boundaries (“nothing inappropriate…took place”). The core meaning is that the band room becomes a sanctioned site where trust, guidance, and safety are simultaneously felt and later re-interpreted, and where being taken seriously by an adult legitimizes the participant’s emerging sense of self."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Becoming through process: a curriculum that values depth over product",
      "description": "Texturally, the participant recalls being shaped by a program that incorporated theory, listening/watching, and comprehensive peer teaching—elements now recognized (as an educator) as unusually formative. Structurally, this depth is contrasted with other teachers and environments “worried about the end product,” positioning the high-school band as a learning ecology where process is intentionally built into the culture. The experience reconciles past and present: what was once simply ‘band’ becomes, in retrospect, an apprenticeship into musicianship and teaching, revealing how expertise is cultivated through layered practices rather than performance alone."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "A place where striving is seen: perfectionism met with meaningful recognition",
      "description": "Texturally, marching band is described as the first arena where the participant’s drive to excel—named explicitly as perfectionism—was rewarded not only for outcomes but for visible effort. Structurally, this meaning emerges by contrast: academics and other extracurriculars are experienced as flattening distinctions (no difference between “A” and “A+,” effort less tangible), whereas band offers a social language for acknowledging both labor and achievement. The core dimension is an identity-level affirmation: striving becomes not merely private pressure but a socially recognized, shared pursuit that makes excellence feel attainable and worth the cost."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Belonging that steadies transition: peer kinship as a protective home-base",
      "description": "Texturally, college marching band is lived as finding people who “had my back,” forming friendships that persist into adulthood through ordinary intimacy (bonfires, cooking talk, pancake breakfasts). Structurally, this belonging gains its force from the context of displacement—moving from a small town to a new university before classes begin—where fear and uncertainty make a “safe place” especially potent. The experience functions as an interpersonal anchor: shared rehearsal life and social rituals convert an unfamiliar institution into a navigable world, enabling confidence and comfort during the vulnerable first year."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "World-expansion through collective opportunity: travel as shared becoming",
      "description": "Texturally, extravagant trips (e.g., Singapore and Southeast Asia) are carried forward as vivid memories of performing abroad, connecting with others through music, and traveling with newly formed friends. Structurally, these experiences are enabled by the collective infrastructure of the band—large-group travel that becomes financially and logistically possible “for a relatively affordable price.” The meaning is not simply “travel,” but an expansion of horizons that is inseparable from communal participation: the participant’s world grows because the ensemble’s world is mobile, and identity is stretched through shared representation, novelty, and memory-making."
    },
    {
      "theme_name": "Collective excellence that tests and remakes the self: sacrifice, perseverance, and redefined perfection",
      "description": "Texturally, drum corps is narrated as both exalted and bruising: entry into “the best of the best,” fruitful leadership meetings charged by shared dedication, moving moments of voluntary service for “the greater good,” and also tears, self-doubt, and questioned self-worth. Structurally, the intensity comes from an environment where everyone is “equally dedicated to the end goal,” producing a rare form of collective accountability (“you succeed or fail as a whole”) that is difficult to replicate elsewhere. Whole-part reconciliation occurs as the participant integrates all three marching worlds into a central lesson: perfection is acknowledged as unattainable, yet disciplined work reliably produces growth; perseverance becomes an enacted choice rather than a trait; and the marching arts emerge as a synthesis of soft skills, content mastery, and interpersonal coordination that ultimately “shaped me and my values as an adult” within the broader community."
    }
  ]
}

